  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Scarlet waxcrust (Phlebia coccineofulva) is a striking bright red to orange fungus belonging to the Meruliaceae family, characterized by its waxy, crusty appearance on decaying wood. This wood-decomposing fungus plays an important ecological role in breaking down dead hardwoods in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Distinctive Color",
      "description": "Bright scarlet to orange-red coloration that is immediately eye-catching and persists even when dried",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Waxy Texture",
      "description": "Surface has a distinctive waxy, crusty, or gelatinous appearance that feels slippery when moist",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Pattern",
      "description": "Forms thin, irregular crusts or patches tightly adhered to hardwood substrates, often spreading across wood surfaces",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Scarlet waxcrust grows on the decaying wood of dead hardwoods and logs in moist, shaded forest environments throughout New England, particularly in areas with high humidity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The bright red pigmentation of Phlebia coccineofulva may serve as a visual warning to insects and other organisms, as the fungus produces compounds that can inhibit growth of competing microorganisms.",
